# Responsive Figma to Liquid CSS

**Owner:** stitch
**Purpose:** Convert Figma's auto-layout + constraints into mobile-first responsive CSS for Liquid sections.

## Breakpoint Standard

```
Mobile-first base styles → no media query
md: ≥768px → @media (min-width: 768px)
lg: ≥1024px → @media (min-width: 1024px)
xl: ≥1280px → @media (min-width: 1280px)
2xl: ≥1536px → @media (min-width: 1536px)
```

Match Tailwind's defaults so loom can use Tailwind utilities consistently.

## Figma Constraints → CSS Mapping

| Figma constraint | CSS |
|---|---|
| Hug contents | `width: max-content` or `width: auto` |
| Fixed width | `width: {value}px` |
| Fill container | `width: 100%` or flex: 1 |
| Auto-layout vertical | `display: flex; flex-direction: column` |
| Auto-layout horizontal | `display: flex; flex-direction: row` |
| Auto-layout gap | `gap: {value}px` |
| Auto-layout padding | `padding: {top}px {right}px {bottom}px {left}px` |
| Align: top + center | `align-items: flex-start; justify-content: center` |
| Distribute: space-between | `justify-content: space-between` |

## Responsive Frame Detection

In Figma, designers typically deliver:
1. Desktop frame (1440px or 1280px width)
2. Mobile frame (375px width)
3. Sometimes tablet (768px or 1024px)

Stitch's job: derive intermediate behavior + ensure no layout breaks between breakpoints.

## Conversion Examples

### Hero section (Figma: full-width, padding 80px top desktop / 40px mobile)

```css
.hero {
  padding: 40px var(--space-md);
}

@media (min-width: 768px) {
  .hero {
    padding: 80px var(--space-lg);
  }
}
```

### Grid (Figma: 4-col desktop, 2-col tablet, 1-col mobile)

```css
.product-grid {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: 1fr;
  gap: var(--space-md);
}

@media (min-width: 768px) {
  .product-grid {
    grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr);
  }
}

@media (min-width: 1024px) {
  .product-grid {
    grid-template-columns: repeat(4, 1fr);
  }
}
```

## Anti-Patterns

- ❌ Never use `min-width: 1px` media query (use mobile-first base)
- ❌ Never use desktop-first breakpoints (`max-width: 1023px`)
- ❌ Never hardcode pixel values — use CSS variables
- ❌ Never skip mobile breakpoint (Figma might not show it; design backwards if needed)
- ❌ Never use Figma absolute positions blindly — convert to flex/grid

## Special Cases

- **Sticky header on scroll** → `position: sticky; top: 0` + `z-index: 50`
- **Cart drawer overlay** → `position: fixed; inset: 0; z-index: 100`
- **Image aspect ratio preservation** → `aspect-ratio: 16 / 9` + `object-fit: cover`
- **Container max-width** → wrap content in `.container { max-width: var(--container-max); margin: 0 auto; padding: 0 var(--gutter) }`

<!-- AUTHORING TODO: Capture frequent layout patterns (hero with split image, product grid, testimonials carousel) as reusable CSS templates after first 5 projects. -->

